{"meta":{"course":"statics","chapter":"05","section":"01","chapter_title":"Centroids and Moments of Inertia","title":"Module 1: Centroids, Distributed Loads, and Moments of Inertia","description":"Compute centroids of composite shapes, replace distributed loads with equivalent point loads, and find area moments of inertia using the parallel-axis theorem and composite-bodies method. Bridge to mass moment of inertia for dynamics.","learning_objectives":["Locate the centroid of a composite area using the area-weighted average (with negative areas for cutouts).","Replace a distributed load along a beam with an equivalent point load at the load's centroid.","Compute area moments of inertia for composite shapes using the parallel-axis theorem.","Distinguish area moment of inertia (m⁴, statics bending) from mass moment of inertia (kg·m², dynamics rotation)."],"concept_ids":["centroid","center_of_mass","distributed_load","moment_of_inertia","parallel_axis_theorem","composite_bodies"],"prereq_section_ids":["statics/04/01","statics/02/03"],"practice_ids":["moi_rectangle_base","moi_centroid_minimum","moi_units_distinction","moi_distributed_to_point","moi_rectangle_base_numeric"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1},"content":"# Centroids, Distributed Loads, and Moments of Inertia\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Locate** the centroid of a composite area using the area-weighted average, with negative areas for cutouts\n2. **Replace** a distributed load along a beam with an equivalent point load at the load's centroid\n3. **Compute** area moments of inertia for composite shapes using the parallel-axis theorem and composite-bodies method\n4. **Distinguish** area moment of inertia ($\\text{m}^4$, statics bending) from mass moment of inertia ($\\text{kg·m}^2$, dynamics rotation)\n\n---\n\n## Opening: Why an I-Beam Beats a Square\n\nTake two steel beams that use the *exact same amount of material* per meter of length. One is a solid square; the other rolls that material into an I-shape, pushing most of it into a top and bottom flange far from the center. Load them identically and the I-beam deflects a fraction as much and carries far more before it yields. Nothing changed but *where* the area sits.\n\nThat single fact, that bending resistance depends on the distribution of area and not just its amount, is why centroids and moments of inertia exist as a topic. The centroid tells you where the area's \"first moment\" balances; the moment of inertia (the \"second moment\") tells you how effectively that area resists bending about an axis. Engineers spend the rest of their careers exploiting the gap between the I-beam and the square.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Every cross section you will ever size in Mechanics of Materials enters the stress formula as a moment of inertia. This section is where you learn to compute it.\n\n---\n\n## Why Distributed Quantities Need Their Own Toolkit\n\nUp to this chapter, statics has lived in the world of point forces and rigid-body equilibrium. Real engineering geometry is not concentrated at points. The weight of a beam is distributed along its length. The pressure on a dam is distributed across its face. The mass of a rotating shaft is distributed across its cross section. To analyze these systems, you replace the distribution with a **single equivalent point** that captures its first moment (centroid) or its second moment (moment of inertia).\n\nThis module covers the four computations every undergraduate statics student should be able to do: locating a centroid, locating a center of mass, computing an area moment of inertia, and applying the parallel-axis theorem. Beer and Johnston devote two chapters (5 and 9) to this material; Hibbeler devotes Ch. 9. Their structure is the structure of this module.\n\n---\n\n## Centroid: The First-Moment Average of Geometry\n\nThe **centroid** of an area is the geometric average of every point in the region, weighted by area. For an area A in the xy-plane,\n\n$$\\bar{x} = \\frac{1}{A}\\int_A x\\,dA, \\quad \\bar{y} = \\frac{1}{A}\\int_A y\\,dA$$\n\nFor composite shapes (rectangle + triangle + circle, with cutouts), use the **composite-bodies method**: list the constituent shapes, their individual centroids, and their individual areas (negative for cutouts), then weight-average.\n\n$$\\bar{x} = \\frac{\\sum_i A_i \\bar{x}_i}{\\sum_i A_i}, \\quad \\bar{y} = \\frac{\\sum_i A_i \\bar{y}_i}{\\sum_i A_i}$$\n\nThe centroid is a property of geometry only. It does not depend on the material's density.\n\n---\n\n## Center of Mass and Center of Gravity\n\nThe **center of mass** weights position by mass, not area:\n\n$$\\bar{x}_{\\rm cm} = \\frac{\\int x\\,dm}{\\int dm} = \\frac{\\int x\\,\\rho\\,dV}{\\int \\rho\\,dV}$$\n\nFor a body with uniform density, the center of mass coincides with the centroid of the volume. For a body with variable density (composite materials, gradient layers), the two differ, and you must use the mass-weighted form.\n\nThe **center of gravity** further weights by the local gravitational field. On Earth's surface, gravity is essentially uniform, so center of gravity coincides with center of mass. For systems extending across significant gravitational gradients (orbital structures), the two diverge.\n\nFor static equilibrium of beams, plates, and machine parts, the center of mass is the point at which the body's weight can be replaced by a single concentrated force without changing equilibrium.\n\n---\n\n## Distributed Loads: Replace by Equivalent Point Load\n\nA distributed load w(x) along a beam can be replaced by an equivalent concentrated force whose magnitude is the area under the load curve, applied at the centroid of that area:\n\n$$F_{\\rm eq} = \\int_0^L w(x)\\,dx, \\quad x_{\\rm eq} = \\frac{\\int_0^L x\\,w(x)\\,dx}{F_{\\rm eq}}$$\n\nFor a uniform distributed load $w_0$ over length L, $F_{\\rm eq} = w_0 L$ at the midpoint. For a triangular load that ramps from 0 to $w_0$ over length L, $F_{\\rm eq} = w_0 L / 2$ at $x = 2L/3$ from the zero end.\n\nThis replacement is the bridge between \"the beam carries 5 kN/m\" and the equilibrium equations you can actually solve.\n\n---\n\n## Area Moment of Inertia: The Second Moment\n\nThe **area moment of inertia** about an axis is the integral of squared distance to that axis, weighted by area:\n\n$$I_x = \\int_A y^2\\,dA, \\quad I_y = \\int_A x^2\\,dA$$\n\nThis is sometimes called the *second moment of area* to avoid confusion with the *mass moment of inertia* (rotational inertia in dynamics). For a rectangle of width b and height h about its base,\n\n$$I_{\\rm base} = \\frac{bh^3}{3}$$\n\nAbout its centroidal axis,\n\n$$I_{\\rm c} = \\frac{bh^3}{12}$$\n\nThe area moment of inertia is the geometric quantity that determines how a beam resists bending. Higher $I$ means stiffer beam, lower stress for the same load. This is why I-beams have most of their material far from the neutral axis: they maximize $I$ for a given amount of material.\n\nNotice the cubic dependence on height: $I_c = bh^3/12$. Doubling the width $b$ doubles $I$, but doubling the height $h$ multiplies $I$ by eight. Drag the sliders to feel how steeply height dominates bending stiffness, and watch the base value track $bh^3/3$.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Rectangle Moment of Inertia: width vs height (note the cube on h)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Width b\",\"min\":10,\"max\":120,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"mm\",\"default\":60},{\"name\":\"h\",\"label\":\"Height h\",\"min\":10,\"max\":200,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"mm\",\"default\":120}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Centroidal I_c = b h^3 / 12\",\"unit\":\"mm^4\",\"expr\":\"b*h^3/12\"},{\"label\":\"Base I = b h^3 / 3\",\"unit\":\"mm^4\",\"expr\":\"b*h^3/3\"},{\"label\":\"Area A = b h\",\"unit\":\"mm^2\",\"expr\":\"b*h\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"h\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A designer needs more bending stiffness from a rectangular beam but can only add material in one direction. Which change buys the most extra I_c?\" explanation=\"Because I_c = bh^3/12, height enters as a cube while width enters linearly. Increasing h by 50% multiplies I by 1.5^3 = 3.375; increasing b by 50% only multiplies I by 1.5. Adding depth wins decisively, which is exactly why beams are oriented with their tall dimension vertical.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Increase the height h by 50%.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Increase the width b by 50%.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Either change adds the same I.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"moment-of-inertia-shapes\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## Parallel-Axis Theorem\n\nA central tool: the moment of inertia about any axis equals the moment of inertia about the **parallel centroidal axis** plus a correction:\n\n$$I = I_{\\rm c} + A d^2$$\n\nwhere d is the distance between the two parallel axes. This is the *parallel-axis theorem* (sometimes called the Steiner theorem for masses).\n\n### A Short Derivation\n\nThe theorem is not an axiom; it falls out of the definition in two lines. Let the centroidal axis be at $y = 0$ and the new parallel axis be a distance $d$ away, so a strip at centroidal coordinate $y$ sits at distance $(y + d)$ from the new axis. By definition,\n\n$$I = \\int_A (y + d)^2\\,dA = \\int_A y^2\\,dA + 2d\\int_A y\\,dA + d^2\\int_A dA$$\n\nExamine the three terms. The first is $\\int_A y^2\\,dA = I_{\\rm c}$, the centroidal moment of inertia. The middle term contains $\\int_A y\\,dA$, the *first moment of area about the centroidal axis*, which is zero by the very definition of the centroid (that is what makes it the centroid). The last term is $d^2 \\int_A dA = A d^2$. The cross term vanishing is the entire reason the formula is so clean:\n\n$$I = I_{\\rm c} + 0 + A d^2 = I_{\\rm c} + A d^2$$\n\nBecause $A d^2 \\ge 0$, the centroidal value $I_{\\rm c}$ is necessarily the smallest moment of inertia among all parallel axes. The same algebra, with $dA$ replaced by $dm$, produces the mass version $I = I_{\\rm cm} + M d^2$.\n\nThe transfer term $A d^2$ is what makes flanges so powerful: it grows with the *square* of the offset. Hold the piece fixed and slide the offset $d$ to watch how quickly the transferred inertia overwhelms the small centroidal contribution.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Parallel-Axis Transfer: I = I_c + A d^2 for one rectangular piece\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"Ic\",\"label\":\"Centroidal I_c of piece\",\"min\":1000,\"max\":500000,\"step\":1000,\"unit\":\"mm^4\",\"default\":8333},{\"name\":\"A\",\"label\":\"Piece area A\",\"min\":100,\"max\":2000,\"step\":50,\"unit\":\"mm^2\",\"default\":1000},{\"name\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Offset d to reference axis\",\"min\":0,\"max\":100,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"mm\",\"default\":45}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Transfer term A d^2\",\"unit\":\"mm^4\",\"expr\":\"A*d^2\"},{\"label\":\"Total I = I_c + A d^2\",\"unit\":\"mm^4\",\"expr\":\"Ic+A*d^2\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"d\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nTwo consequences. First, the centroidal axis is always the axis of *minimum* I (the $Ad^2$ term is non-negative). Second, the theorem makes composite-shape problems tractable: compute each piece's centroidal I, then transfer to a common axis with $Ad^2$, and sum.\n\nFor composite shapes (I-beam built from three rectangles, or a circle with a hole) the workflow is:\n\n1. Pick a common reference axis.\n2. For each piece, compute centroidal $I_i$ from the standard tables.\n3. Compute the offset $d_i$ from each piece's centroid to the reference axis.\n4. Sum $I_{\\rm total} = \\sum_i (I_i + A_i d_i^2)$, with cutouts entering as negative $A_i$.\n\nThis is the workflow. It does not change with shape; only the table values change.\n\nReorder the steps below into the correct composite-bodies sequence. The reference axis must be chosen before offsets can be measured, and the summation comes last.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Pick a common reference axis\",\"Look up each piece&apos;s centroidal I_i from standard tables\",\"Measure each offset d_i from the piece centroid to the reference axis\",\"Compute each transfer term A_i d_i^2 (cutouts use negative A_i)\",\"Sum I_total = sum of (I_i + A_i d_i^2)\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n---\n\n## Mass Moment of Inertia: The Dynamics Bridge\n\nIn dynamics, the **mass moment of inertia** plays the role mass plays in linear dynamics:\n\n$$I_{\\rm mass} = \\int r^2\\,dm$$\n\nIt governs how torque produces angular acceleration ($\\tau = I\\alpha$). Physically, it is the body's resistance to changes in rotational motion. Like its area cousin, it has a parallel-axis theorem:\n\n$$I = I_{\\rm cm} + Md^2$$\n\nFor a slender rod of length L and mass M about its centroid, $I_{\\rm cm} = ML^2/12$. About one end, $I_{\\rm end} = ML^2/3$. The factor-of-four difference between the two reflects the parallel-axis correction.\n\nFor undergraduate statics, the area moment of inertia is the working concept. The mass moment of inertia returns in dynamics chapters on rigid-body rotation.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Examples\n\n### Example 1: Centroid of a Rectangle with a Circular Hole\n\n**Problem:** A rectangular plate is 120 mm wide and 80 mm tall, with the origin at its lower-left corner. A circular hole of radius 20 mm is drilled with its center at $(90, 40)$ mm. Find the centroid $(\\bar{x}, \\bar{y})$ of the remaining material.\n\n**Step 1: List the pieces** (the hole enters with *negative* area).\n\n| Piece | Area $A_i$ (mm²) | $\\bar{x}_i$ (mm) | $\\bar{y}_i$ (mm) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Full rectangle | $120 \\times 80 = 9600$ | 60 | 40 |\n| Hole (negative) | $-\\pi (20)^2 = -1256.6$ | 90 | 40 |\n\n**Step 2: Total area.**\n\n$$A = 9600 - 1256.6 = 8343.4 \\text{ mm}^2$$\n\n**Step 3: Area-weighted average.**\n\n$$\\bar{x} = \\frac{(9600)(60) + (-1256.6)(90)}{8343.4} = \\frac{576000 - 113094}{8343.4} = \\boxed{55.48 \\text{ mm}}$$\n\n$$\\bar{y} = \\frac{(9600)(40) + (-1256.6)(40)}{8343.4} = \\boxed{40.0 \\text{ mm}}$$\n\nThe hole pulls the centroid from $x = 60$ mm toward the solid side, landing at $55.48$ mm. The $y$-coordinate stays at 40 mm because the hole sits on the horizontal centerline. Removing material always shifts the centroid *away* from the removed region.\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Rectangle Moment of Inertia, Centroidal and About the Base\n\n**Problem:** A rectangular cross section has width $b = 60$ mm and height $h = 120$ mm. Find the area moment of inertia about its horizontal centroidal axis and about its base.\n\n**Step 1: Centroidal axis** (standard result $I_c = bh^3/12$).\n\n$$I_c = \\frac{(60)(120)^3}{12} = \\frac{60 \\times 1{,}728{,}000}{12} = \\boxed{8.64 \\times 10^6 \\text{ mm}^4}$$\n\n**Step 2: About the base, by the parallel-axis theorem** $I = I_c + A d^2$, with $d = h/2 = 60$ mm and $A = bh = 7200$ mm²:\n\n$$I_{\\text{base}} = 8.64 \\times 10^6 + (7200)(60)^2 = 8.64 \\times 10^6 + 25.92 \\times 10^6 = \\boxed{34.56 \\times 10^6 \\text{ mm}^4}$$\n\nAs a check, the closed form is $I_{\\text{base}} = bh^3/3 = (60)(120)^3/3 = 34.56 \\times 10^6$ mm⁴. They agree, and $I_{\\text{base}} = 4 I_c$ exactly, the signature of the $Ad^2$ correction for a rectangle.\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: I-Beam Moment of Inertia vs. a Solid Square\n\n**Problem:** An I-beam has a top flange 100 mm × 10 mm, a bottom flange 100 mm × 10 mm, and a web 10 mm × 80 mm, total height 100 mm. Compute the centroidal moment of inertia $I_x$ about the horizontal axis, and compare it to a solid square of equal area.\n\n**Step 1: Centroid.** By symmetry, the centroidal axis is at mid-height, $\\bar{y} = 50$ mm.\n\n**Step 2: Composite-bodies + parallel axis.** Measure each piece's centroid from the bottom: top flange at $y = 95$, web at $y = 50$, bottom flange at $y = 5$.\n\n| Piece | $I_{c,i} = bh^3/12$ (mm⁴) | $A_i$ (mm²) | $d_i$ (mm) | $A_i d_i^2$ (mm⁴) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top flange | $100(10)^3/12 = 8{,}333$ | 1000 | 45 | $2{,}025{,}000$ |\n| Web | $10(80)^3/12 = 426{,}667$ | 800 | 0 | 0 |\n| Bottom flange | $8{,}333$ | 1000 | 45 | $2{,}025{,}000$ |\n\n**Step 3: Sum.**\n\n$$I_x = (8333 + 426667 + 8333) + (2025000 + 0 + 2025000) = \\boxed{4.49 \\times 10^6 \\text{ mm}^4}$$\n\n**Step 4: Compare to a solid square of equal area.** Total area $= 1000 + 800 + 1000 = 2800$ mm², so the square side is $s = \\sqrt{2800} = 52.9$ mm, giving $I_{\\text{sq}} = s^4/12 = 0.65 \\times 10^6$ mm⁴.\n\nThe I-beam delivers about **seven times** the bending stiffness of a square using the same material, purely by moving area to the flanges where $d^2$ is large. This is Example 0's \"why I-beams win\" made quantitative.\n\nWork the I-beam solution one decision at a time. Predict each step before revealing the result.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Building the I-Beam I_x step by step\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Where does the centroidal axis sit for this symmetric I-beam (total height 100 mm)?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"At the bottom flange, y = 5 mm\",\"At mid-height, y = 50 mm\",\"At the top flange, y = 95 mm\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"By top-bottom symmetry the centroid is at mid-height, y = 50 mm. The web centroid coincides with this axis, so the web has zero offset (d = 0).\"},{\"prompt\":\"What is the offset d for each flange from the mid-height axis?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"10 mm\",\"45 mm\",\"50 mm\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Each flange centroid is 45 mm from mid-height: the flange center sits at 95 mm (top) or 5 mm (bottom), so d = 95 - 50 = 45 mm. The transfer term per flange is A d^2 = 1000 x 45^2 = 2,025,000 mm^4.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Which contribution dominates the web&apos;s total I: its own centroidal I_c or its transfer term?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Its transfer term A d^2\",\"Its centroidal I_c\",\"They are equal\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The web sits on the axis (d = 0), so its transfer term is zero. Its entire contribution is the centroidal I_c = 10(80)^3/12 = 426,667 mm^4, the largest single centroidal term.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Summing: I_x = (8333 + 426667 + 8333) + (2025000 + 0 + 2025000) = 4.49 x 10^6 mm^4. Against the equal-area square (s = 52.9 mm, I = 0.65 x 10^6 mm^4), the I-beam is about 7x stiffer using identical material.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"moment-of-inertia-shapes\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: Equivalent Point Load for a Triangular Distribution\n\n**Problem:** A beam carries a triangular distributed load that ramps from 0 at the left to $w_0 = 6$ kN/m at the right over a length $L = 4$ m. Find the magnitude and location of the equivalent point load.\n\n**Step 1: Magnitude = area under the load curve** (area of the triangle).\n\n$$F_{\\text{eq}} = \\frac{1}{2} w_0 L = \\frac{1}{2}(6)(4) = \\boxed{12 \\text{ kN}}$$\n\n**Step 2: Location = centroid of the triangle.** For a triangle with its apex (peak) at the right end, the centroid sits $2L/3$ from the zero end:\n\n$$x_{\\text{eq}} = \\frac{2L}{3} = \\frac{2(4)}{3} = \\boxed{2.67 \\text{ m from the left (zero) end}}$$\n\nThis single 12 kN force, placed 2.67 m from the left, is statically equivalent to the entire distribution for the purpose of finding reactions, the bridge between \"the beam carries a 6 kN/m triangular load\" and the equilibrium equations you can actually solve.\n\nVary the peak intensity and span of the triangular load and watch how the resultant magnitude scales, while the line of action stays locked at $2L/3$ from the zero end regardless of $w_0$.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Triangular Distributed Load: resultant and its line of action\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"w0\",\"label\":\"Peak intensity w_0\",\"min\":1,\"max\":20,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"kN/m\",\"default\":6},{\"name\":\"L\",\"label\":\"Span L\",\"min\":1,\"max\":12,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":4}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Equivalent point load F_eq = w_0 L / 2\",\"unit\":\"kN\",\"expr\":\"w0*L/2\"},{\"label\":\"Location x_eq = 2L/3 from zero end\",\"unit\":\"m\",\"expr\":\"2*L/3\"},{\"label\":\"Overturning moment about zero end F_eq * x_eq\",\"unit\":\"kN*m\",\"expr\":\"(w0*L/2)*(2*L/3)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"L\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n---\n\n## Common Errors and Their Diagnoses\n\n- **Confusing area moment with mass moment.** Both have the symbol $I$ in different contexts. Read the units: area moment is m⁴, mass moment is kg·m².\n- **Forgetting the parallel-axis correction.** When summing composite pieces, the centroidal $I_i$ is about each piece's own centroid; you must add $A_i d_i^2$ to bring it to the common axis.\n- **Reversing the sign convention on cutouts.** A circle removed from a rectangle enters with negative area (and zero $I$ from the cutout's centroidal contribution, plus negative $A d^2$). Forgetting this overestimates I.\n- **Using the centroid as the center of mass for non-uniform density.** The two coincide only for uniform density. For composite materials, weight by mass.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Centroids and Moments of Inertia\" context=\"An I-beam has flange dimensions 100 mm × 10 mm (top), 100 mm × 10 mm (bottom), and a web 10 mm × 80 mm. Compute the centroidal moment of inertia about the horizontal axis using the composite-bodies method and the parallel-axis theorem. Compare with a solid 100 mm × 100 mm square of the same total area to show why I-beams resist bending more efficiently.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\nThe three quantities in this section (centroid, equivalent load, moment of inertia) are not academic exercises; each one feeds directly into a downstream design calculation an engineer cannot skip.\n\nWalk a real sizing decision: a floor joist deflects too much. Choose how to fix it and see the consequences of each path.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"A floor joist deflects too much: how do you stiffen it?\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"A simply supported timber joist sags beyond the allowable limit under its distributed floor load. You may change the cross section but must keep material cost roughly fixed. What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reorient the same rectangular section so the tall dimension is vertical\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Smart first check. Since I_c = bh^3/12, putting the larger dimension as the height h (cubed) maximizes I for free.\"},{\"label\":\"Add width b, keeping height h the same\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"This helps only linearly. Width enters I to the first power, so you spend a lot of material for little stiffness.\"},{\"label\":\"Switch to a built-up I-section of equal area\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Aggressive but effective: moving area to flanges raises I via the A d^2 transfer terms.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"Reorienting helped but you are still slightly over the deflection limit. Material budget is nearly the same. Next?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Move some area to top and bottom flanges (toward an I-shape)\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Correct instinct. The parallel-axis A d^2 term rewards area placed far from the neutral axis.\"},{\"label\":\"Increase width further\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Still only linear payoff. You are leaving the cubic-in-height and squared-in-offset levers on the table.\"}]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"Stiffness barely improved per kilogram added. What does the math tell you to do instead?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Exploit height (cubed) and offset (squared), i.e. go to a deeper or flanged section\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. The two big levers are h^3 in I_c and d^2 in the transfer term, not width.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"You adopt an I-section: most area in flanges far from the neutral axis. Result?\",\"feedback\":\"Deflection drops sharply. For the same area, the flanges sit at large d, so each contributes A d^2 to I. This is the I-beam advantage from Example 3 (roughly 7x a solid square), now applied to a real joist. Deflection scales as 1/I, so a 7x I cuts sag to about one-seventh.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n| Application | Quantity Used | Why It Matters |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Beam selection (steel W-shapes) | Area moment of inertia $I$ | Deflection $\\delta \\propto 1/I$ and bending stress $\\sigma = Mc/I$; sizing a floor joist or crane girder starts here |\n| Welded plate girders and aircraft spars | Composite $I$ via parallel axis | Built-up sections push area to the flanges to maximize $I$ per kilogram of material |\n| Retaining walls and dam faces | Equivalent point load of a triangular pressure | Hydrostatic pressure grows linearly with depth; the resultant acts at $2L/3$ down from the surface, setting the overturning moment |\n| Snow and wind loads on roofs | Equivalent point load of a distributed load | Code loads are given as kN/m²; reactions need the resultant and its line of action |\n| Rotating shafts and flywheels | Mass moment of inertia $I_{\\rm mass}$ | $\\tau = I\\alpha$ sizes the motor torque and sets the stored energy $\\tfrac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$ |\n| Machine-part balancing | Center of mass | An unbalanced center of mass off the spin axis creates a rotating force that fatigues bearings |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** When a structural engineer opens a steel manual and reads off $I_x = 4.49 \\times 10^6\\ \\text{mm}^4$ for a section, that number was produced by exactly the composite-bodies and parallel-axis procedure in Example 3. The table just saves you from recomputing it each time.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Composite centroid | $\\bar{x} = \\dfrac{\\sum A_i \\bar{x}_i}{\\sum A_i}$ |\n| Equivalent point load | $F_{\\text{eq}} = \\displaystyle\\int w(x)\\,dx$ at the load's centroid |\n| Rectangle (centroidal) | $I_c = bh^3/12$ |\n| Rectangle (base) | $I_{\\text{base}} = bh^3/3$ |\n| Parallel-axis theorem | $I = I_c + A d^2$ |\n\n### How the Ideas Connect\n\nThese six concepts form one chain: the centroid (first moment) locates where area balances, which is also where a distributed load resolves to a point and the axis about which the moment of inertia (second moment) is minimized; the parallel-axis theorem then transfers that inertia, and the composite-bodies method assembles real cross sections from the pieces.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"centroid\",\"center_of_mass\",\"distributed_load\",\"moment_of_inertia\",\"parallel_axis_theorem\",\"composite_bodies\"]'></concept-map>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why the parallel-axis theorem guarantees that the centroidal axis is the axis of minimum moment of inertia, and connect this to why I-beams place material in the flanges.\" answer=\"Because I = I_c + A d^2 and the term A d^2 is never negative (A is positive area, d^2 is squared distance), any non-centroidal axis adds a strictly positive amount to I_c, so I_c is the smallest possible value. The centroidal first moment integral equals zero, which is what kills the cross term in the derivation. I-beams exploit the opposite direction of this same fact: by placing flange area at large distance d from the neutral axis, each flange contributes a large A d^2, maximizing the section I for a fixed amount of material.\"></self-explain>\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- The centroid is the area-weighted average of geometry; cutouts enter with negative area.\n- A distributed load is equivalent to a point load equal to the area under the curve, applied at that area's centroid.\n- The area moment of inertia ($\\text{m}^4$) governs bending stiffness; the mass moment of inertia ($\\text{kg·m}^2$) governs rotational dynamics.\n- The parallel-axis theorem $I = I_c + Ad^2$ makes the centroidal axis the minimum-$I$ axis.\n- Pushing area far from the neutral axis (the I-beam strategy) maximizes $I$ for a fixed amount of material.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn **Chapter 6: Structural Analysis**, we turn from cross-section properties back to whole structures, analyzing trusses member by member with the method of joints. The equilibrium habits from Chapters 1 to 4 and the centroid reasoning from this chapter both reappear there.\n\n---\n\n### Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A rectangle of width b and height h has its centroidal moment of inertia about the horizontal centroidal axis equal to bh³/12. About the base, the moment of inertia is:\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"bh³/12 (same as centroidal).\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"bh³/3 (parallel-axis correction: bh³/12 + bh × (h/2)² = bh³/12 + bh³/4 = bh³/3).\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"bh³/4.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. The parallel-axis theorem gives I_base = I_c + A d² = bh³/12 + bh × (h/2)² = bh³/3. The centroidal axis always minimizes I.\"></interactive-quiz>\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"moi_rectangle_base","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"parallel_axis_theorem","concept_ids":["parallel_axis_theorem","moment_of_inertia"],"stem":"A rectangle has width b and height h. Its centroidal area moment of inertia is bh³/12. What is its moment of inertia about the base?","options":["bh³/12 (same as centroidal).","bh³/3 (centroidal value plus the parallel-axis correction A × d² = bh × (h/2)² = bh³/4).","bh³/2.","bh³/24."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Parallel-axis theorem: I_base = I_c + A × d² = bh³/12 + bh × (h/2)² = bh³/12 + bh³/4 = bh³/3.","misconception_id":"no_parallel_axis_correction"},{"id":"moi_centroid_minimum","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"moment_of_inertia","concept_ids":["moment_of_inertia","parallel_axis_theorem","centroid"],"stem":"Among all axes parallel to a given direction, which axis through the body has the minimum moment of inertia?","options":["Any axis at the boundary.","The centroidal axis. The parallel-axis theorem I = I_c + Ad² makes the Ad² term non-negative, so I_c is the minimum.","The axis farthest from the centroid.","All parallel axes give the same I."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Ad² is non-negative, so any non-centroidal parallel axis adds to I_c. The centroidal axis is uniquely the minimum.","misconception_id":"any_axis_same_i"},{"id":"moi_units_distinction","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"moment_of_inertia","concept_ids":["moment_of_inertia"],"stem":"Which units distinguish the area moment of inertia from the mass moment of inertia?","options":["Both have units of m⁴.","Area moment of inertia: m⁴ (geometric, used in statics for bending). Mass moment of inertia: kg·m² (dynamics, used in τ = Iα). Reading the units prevents confusing the two.","Both have units of kg·m².","Neither has units."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Different physical quantities sharing the symbol I. Units disambiguate them.","misconception_id":"two_inertias_same"},{"id":"moi_distributed_to_point","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"distributed_load","concept_ids":["distributed_load","centroid"],"stem":"A triangular distributed load ramps from 0 at one end to w₀ at the other end of a beam of length L. The equivalent concentrated force is:","options":["w₀L at the midpoint.","w₀L/2 (the area under the triangle) acting at 2L/3 from the zero end (the centroid of a triangle with apex at the zero end).","w₀L at 2L/3.","w₀L/2 at the midpoint."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The equivalent force magnitude equals the area under the load curve; its location is the centroid of that area. For a triangle: F = w₀L/2 at x = 2L/3.","misconception_id":"uniform_substitute_for_triangular"},{"id":"moi_rectangle_base_numeric","type":"numeric","difficulty":"medium","statement":"A rectangular cross section has width b = 80 mm and height h = 160 mm. Compute the area moment of inertia about its base (an axis along the bottom edge). Report the answer in 10^6 mm^4.","givens":{"width":"80 mm","height":"160 mm"},"final_answer":{"value":109.23,"unit":"10^6 mm^4","tolerance":0.05},"concept_ids":["moment_of_inertia","parallel_axis_theorem"],"hint":"For a rectangle about its base, I_base = b*h^3/3 (equivalently I_c + A*(h/2)^2)."}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"d83e3f6615b15af49e10b5dea617d467fd3a77badc716ab5ab8d40f37b2c4151","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}